    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d74">

                <pb facs="0116" n="100" sameAs="frus:frus1951v07p1/pg100"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">795.00/1–1851: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Gifford</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                        <lb/>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">
                            niact
                        </hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1951-01-18T17:00:00+00:00">January 18, 1951—5
                            p. m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>3985. Embtel 3975, January 18;<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d74-f1"/> repeated USUN 69.</p>
                <p>Foreign Office has just telegraphed instructions to UKUN stating UK finds terms Chinese reply to cease-fire resolution
                    unacceptable and authorizing Jebb “have good smack at it”. UKUN informed UK <pb facs="0117" n="101"
                        xml:id="pg101"/> hopes US will not insist on introducing new resolution
                    today. If US does insist, and resolution introduced by USUN is along lines described London [<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Department</hi>] circular 387,<anchor n="2" xml:id="d74-f2"/> UK would wish
                    have it amended. UK would prefer have condemnatory resolution handled separately
                    from any resolution calling for further UN action and not combined in one
                    document.</p>
                <p>Sent Department 3985, repeated info <hi rendition="#small-caps">
                        niact
                    </hi>
                    USUN 61.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gifford</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d74-f1" n="1" xml:id="d74-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed. It reported British reaction that the response of the Chinese
                    Communists to the cease-fire proposals was “most discouraging”, but went on to
                    say that there was general agreement in British Government circles that the
                    United Nations should not be rushed into further action without time for
                    thorough consideration of the consequences. (795.00/1–1851)</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d74-f2" n="2" xml:id="d74-f2-content">
                <p>Dated January 15, <ref target="#pg83">p. 83</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
